    'endoscopically evident perforation, aspiration, anesthesia-related instability, or other immediate adverse event.\n'
    'Estimated blood loss was minimal. A procedure time of 84 minutes reflected the detailed surveillance examination\n'
    'and multiple therapeutic interventions; the patient was transferred to monitored recovery awake and\n'
    'hemodynamically stable.\n'
    'Postprocedure impression\n'
    '1. Short fibrotic-appearing ileocolonic anastomotic stricture in a patient with postoperative Crohn disease,\n'
    'successfully treated with wire-guided through-the-scope balloon dilation to 15 mm, with successful subsequent\n'
    'intubation of the neo-terminal ileum.\n'
    '2. Mild recurrent neo-terminal ileitis with scattered aphthous erosions and mild patchy colonic erythema, sampled by\n'
    'targeted mucosal biopsy at sites separate from the therapeutic polypectomy and mucosal-resection lesions.\n'
    '3. Distal transverse-colon 23 mm granular laterally spreading lesion, treated with saline-assisted piecemeal\n'
    'endoscopic mucosal resection and prophylactic defect closure.\n'
    '4. Distinct 17 mm non-granular splenic-flexure lesion with depressed morphology, intentionally not biopsied or\n'
    'removed; carbon-particle tattoo placed distal and opposite the lesion for accurate future localization.\n'
    '\n'
    '\n'
    'GASTROENTEROLOGY  /  PROCEDURE REPORT\n'
    'NOV 08, 2026  |  ELENA BROOKS\n'
    'Advanced Endoscopy Unit   •   Endoscopist: E. Morgan, MD\n'
    'Page 5\n'
    '5. Mid-descending-colon 9 mm sessile polyp, completely removed by cold-snare technique. Nonbleeding rectal\n'
    'vascular ectasia, mild sigmoid diverticulosis, and small internal hemorrhoids were incidental findings and required no\n'
    'therapy.\n'
    'Recovery instructions and management plan\n'
    'The patient may begin clear liquids after recovery and advance to a low-residue diet as tolerated for the next 48\n'
    'hours, with particular attention to post-dilation discomfort, progressive abdominal distention, or persistent pain. She\n'
